WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.868 --> 00:00:02.000
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.000
is a Grammy-nominated
comedian and actor

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.667
who's currently on
his Dark Pale comedy tour.

00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:09.234
He stars in the new film
"Linoleum,"

00:00:09.234 --> 00:00:10.667
which is in select theaters now.

00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:12.100
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:14.801
-Tony, did you give the Saturday
morning slot to another show?

00:00:14.801 --> 00:00:16.033
-Cam, Cammy, come on in.

00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:18.267
-Shondra said that --
Whoa.

00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:20.033
-Kent Armstrong.

00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:23.400
-But...I-I can't believe
you're even standing right now.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.968
-Excuse me?

00:00:24.968 --> 00:00:26.267
-The crash.

00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:27.801
You crashed your car yesterday.

00:00:27.801 --> 00:00:30.100
-I'm afraid I have no idea
what you're talking about.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.267
-I saw -- I was --
-No, no, no, no.

00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:33.667
Kent just moved to town.
You know him?

00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:36.367
He's a famous --

00:00:36.367 --> 00:00:38.267
Wow, I'm sorry.
It's just...

00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:40.234
you two look a lot alike.

00:00:40.234 --> 00:00:42.501
You know that?
-Not really.

00:00:42.501 --> 00:00:44.467
-Relax, buddy.
It's a compliment.

00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:46.801
-But I don't think we do.
N-Not that much.

00:00:46.801 --> 00:00:50.367
Can I talk to you, Tony, for --
in private?

00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:51.901
-Be my guest.

00:00:51.901 --> 00:00:53.133
Nice to meet you.

00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:54.467
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:56.901
our very good friend
Jim Gaffigan, everybody!

00:00:56.901 --> 00:00:58.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:58.901 --> 00:01:06.100
♪♪

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:13.267
♪♪

00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:14.634
Welcome back!

00:01:14.634 --> 00:01:17.100
-It's always, always fun
to be here.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.534
This is always a great time.

00:01:19.534 --> 00:01:21.601
I feel like I can
really open up here.

00:01:21.601 --> 00:01:23.234
-Yeah.
We feel the same way.

00:01:23.234 --> 00:01:25.300
-It's the best.
I'm so excited.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.467
-You can take that off
if you want.

00:01:27.467 --> 00:01:28.701
-[ Sighs ]

00:01:28.701 --> 00:01:32.367
Uh, I mean, I don't know
if that's necessary.

00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:33.400
-Okay.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:34.701
-I mean, alright, fine.

00:01:34.701 --> 00:01:36.067
I'll take it off. Geez.
-Alright.

00:01:36.067 --> 00:01:37.767
-Oh, Jesus Christ.

00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:41.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:43.400
I mean...

00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:44.934
I mean...

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Is that good enough for you?
-He went from a shirt

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.801
to a sweater.

00:01:50.801 --> 00:01:51.968
-You know what I mean?

00:01:51.968 --> 00:01:56.267
It's like, oh, Seth wants to see
what I look like.

00:01:56.267 --> 00:01:57.667
Alright.
You know what?

00:01:57.667 --> 00:01:59.501
I'm going to take this off.
-You can take it off.

00:01:59.501 --> 00:02:03.467
Alright.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:05.701
-You know, before you think
I'm all paranoid,

00:02:05.701 --> 00:02:09.367
the only reason I was wearing
this is because I have COVID.

00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:12.901
-Oh, well.
-[ Coughs ]

00:02:12.901 --> 00:02:15.868
I mean, actually, I was just --
I do not have it.

00:02:15.868 --> 00:02:18.667
I do not have COVID.
I was just tested.

00:02:18.667 --> 00:02:20.968
I took one of those
online tests.

00:02:20.968 --> 00:02:21.968
[ Laughter ]

00:02:21.968 --> 00:02:26.234
And it said I'm an empath.

00:02:26.234 --> 00:02:27.801
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.801 --> 00:02:29.334
And I have monkeypox.

00:02:29.334 --> 00:02:31.567
-You have mon--
-I don't know.

00:02:31.567 --> 00:02:34.000
Monkeypox kind of sounds
like a kids' cereal.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.100
-Yeah.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.801
-It's like,
"What's the prize inside?"

00:02:36.801 --> 00:02:38.167
"Sex with men."

00:02:38.167 --> 00:02:40.634
[ Laughter ]

00:02:40.634 --> 00:02:41.834
You know what I mean?

00:02:41.834 --> 00:02:43.367
Like, I don't know
if that's a prize.

00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:45.901
I mean, for some,
it would be a great prize.

00:02:45.901 --> 00:02:48.400
-To each their own
as far as prizes go.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:49.601
Yeah, who are we to judge?

00:02:49.601 --> 00:02:51.701
-This pandemic.
-Yeah.

00:02:51.701 --> 00:02:53.467
-Right?
-But you've been traveling,

00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:56.300
which I think is one of
the things people miss the most.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:58.767
-Yeah, I've gotten to the point

00:02:58.767 --> 00:03:03.234
where I kind of miss
the lockdown a little bit.

00:03:03.234 --> 00:03:07.234
I miss the lockdown because,
you know, it's just --

00:03:07.234 --> 00:03:09.234
I don't want this to come out
the wrong way,

00:03:09.234 --> 00:03:12.133
but people are horrible.

00:03:12.133 --> 00:03:15.734
Like, and I mean just, like,
all of them.

00:03:15.734 --> 00:03:17.067
You know?
But that's alright.

00:03:17.067 --> 00:03:18.567
You know, it's alright.

00:03:18.567 --> 00:03:22.467
It's just I just can't
deal with people.

00:03:22.467 --> 00:03:25.400
-Yeah.
-Like, can you --

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.734
I find it difficult
to go to Starbucks,

00:03:28.734 --> 00:03:31.100
and it's not the coffee,

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:34.667
and I understand Starbucks
is a whole experience,

00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:37.667
but, like, that whole
Starbucks experience

00:03:37.667 --> 00:03:39.567
makes me want
to strangle people.

00:03:39.567 --> 00:03:42.133
-Okay.
Which part specifically?

00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:47.501
-Well, it's just I'm convinced
that humans, we can't handle --

00:03:47.501 --> 00:03:49.334
There's too many options
at Starbucks.

00:03:49.334 --> 00:03:50.634
There's way too many --

00:03:50.634 --> 00:03:53.234
We're used to an efficiency
like in fast-food places.

00:03:53.234 --> 00:03:54.801
Like, "I'll have
a number three."

00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:58.000
But in Starbucks, people
turn into spoiled brats.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.067
They're like, "Okay.

00:04:01.067 --> 00:04:07.167
Let's see, what does baby
feel like sipping on?

00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:11.567
Let me get a venti half-caff
caramel macchiato

00:04:11.567 --> 00:04:17.334
with no sugar, no dairy,
and no coffee."

00:04:17.334 --> 00:04:20.100
And the most amazing thing
happens.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.334
Instead of the Starbucks cashier
slapping this person,

00:04:24.334 --> 00:04:27.601
they just give a nod of,
"As you wish."

00:04:27.601 --> 00:04:29.701
But it doesn't end there.

00:04:29.701 --> 00:04:33.033
After you order, then you --
the suffering continues.

00:04:33.033 --> 00:04:34.968
Then you have to wait
in that pickup area.

00:04:34.968 --> 00:04:38.767
That's like the methadone clinic
of Starbucks,

00:04:38.767 --> 00:04:41.033
where, you know,
you have to hang around

00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:43.434
all the other caffeine addicts.

00:04:43.434 --> 00:04:48.467
You know, and you're forced to
listen to Mannheim Steamroller.

00:04:48.467 --> 00:04:51.100
[ Vocalizing ]

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:53.400
You just judge
other people's orders.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.300
They're like, "I have
a hot chocolate for Dan."

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:56.934
You're like, "What a dork."

00:04:56.934 --> 00:04:59.734
[ Laughter ]

00:04:59.734 --> 00:05:01.868
And if Dan's not right there,

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:03.734
you're like,
"We should kill Dan."

00:05:03.734 --> 00:05:04.934
[ Laughter ]

00:05:04.934 --> 00:05:08.167
You know, he's kind of
asking for it, right?

00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:10.200
You just stand there.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:11.901
You know, you can't --

00:05:11.901 --> 00:05:14.167
There's chairs,
but they're all taken

00:05:14.167 --> 00:05:16.667
because Starbucks
is not only a coffee shop,

00:05:16.667 --> 00:05:20.667
it's also an upscale
unemployment office, right?

00:05:20.667 --> 00:05:25.033
Filled with, you know, people
on conference calls, students.

00:05:25.033 --> 00:05:29.000
There's always that one guy that
set up his office in Starbucks.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.300
It's packed.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.067
It's like the opposite
of Dunkin' Donuts.

00:05:33.067 --> 00:05:35.167
You walk in Dunkin' Donuts,

00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:37.934
you feel like you're entering
an active crime scene.

00:05:37.934 --> 00:05:39.467
[ Laughter ]

00:05:39.467 --> 00:05:44.767
It's like the shelves are bare.
There's no employees.

00:05:44.767 --> 00:05:47.834
You're like,
"Are you guys even open?

00:05:47.834 --> 00:05:50.067
Did I break in?"

00:05:50.067 --> 00:05:53.033
So I just --
I can't deal with it anymore.

00:05:53.033 --> 00:05:54.467
-Yeah.
I get it.

00:05:54.467 --> 00:05:55.868
I mean, I will say, I like that.

00:05:55.868 --> 00:05:58.467
You know,
I'm a Dunkin' supporter, fan.

00:05:58.467 --> 00:05:59.834
-I know.
You're from New England.

00:05:59.834 --> 00:06:01.567
-Yeah, there you go, but --

00:06:01.567 --> 00:06:03.501
In and out.
In and out.

00:06:03.501 --> 00:06:05.834
You get your coffee,
and that's all I want out of it.

00:06:05.834 --> 00:06:08.868
-Yeah.
And you also get an STD.

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:10.167
[ Laughter ]

00:06:10.167 --> 00:06:11.868
-'Cause that used to be
two stops.

00:06:11.868 --> 00:06:13.634
That used to be two stops.
-Right, that used to be.

00:06:13.634 --> 00:06:16.501
But in New England...

00:06:16.501 --> 00:06:19.300
you can encounter
a homeless person

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:21.434
who's also a freelance doorman.

00:06:21.434 --> 00:06:25.167
[ Laughter ]

00:06:25.167 --> 00:06:26.667
It is true.

00:06:26.667 --> 00:06:29.200
-A freelance doorman.
-Freelance doorman.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:30.868
-That's very specific
to New England.

00:06:30.868 --> 00:06:33.434
-Yeah, I just want coffee,
and I want to go.

00:06:33.434 --> 00:06:35.634
-Hey, so I have so ask.

00:06:35.634 --> 00:06:37.234
You know, obviously,
you're a family man.

00:06:37.234 --> 00:06:39.901
Five kids, which blows me away.

00:06:39.901 --> 00:06:43.000
But I want to ask, how is
your wonderful wife doing?

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.467
-She's good. She's good.
Well, she had a brain tumor.

00:06:45.467 --> 00:06:49.033
-I do remember that.
-That's not the funny part.

00:06:49.033 --> 00:06:52.100
She had a brain tumor,
and as a result,

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:54.701
she now has a slight
swallowing disorder.

00:06:54.701 --> 00:06:56.300
That's not the funny part.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:07:00.367
But the weird thing is,
is that, you know,

00:07:00.367 --> 00:07:02.400
when I talk about this
in my show,

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:04.801
the audience understandably
gets uptight.

00:07:04.801 --> 00:07:08.567
And it's just a slight
swallowing disorder,

00:07:08.567 --> 00:07:10.133
which is when she eats bread
or pizza,

00:07:10.133 --> 00:07:12.634
it sounds like she's choking,
but she's not.

00:07:12.634 --> 00:07:14.501
It's just how she gets --

00:07:14.501 --> 00:07:16.400
You know, when people
have this disorder,

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:17.901
it's just how
they get bread down.

00:07:17.901 --> 00:07:19.367
-Now, when you say
when you do your shows

00:07:19.367 --> 00:07:20.734
and the audience
gets uncomfortable,

00:07:20.734 --> 00:07:22.167
is it like what's happening
right now?

00:07:22.167 --> 00:07:23.801
-It's very similar to this.

00:07:23.801 --> 00:07:27.601
You know, where people are like,
"Should he bail on this?

00:07:27.601 --> 00:07:29.834
You know,
maybe he should switch.

00:07:29.834 --> 00:07:31.634
Maybe Seth should save him."

00:07:31.634 --> 00:07:34.300
-No.
I want to hear all about it.

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:37.667
-It's just -- But it's
something, you know,

00:07:37.667 --> 00:07:40.868
as a partner,
I've supported her.

00:07:40.868 --> 00:07:41.968
I try not to --

00:07:41.968 --> 00:07:43.367
You know, it's not
that big of a deal.

00:07:43.367 --> 00:07:44.567
It's something
we've gotten used to.

00:07:44.567 --> 00:07:46.167
I try not to draw attention
to her.

00:07:46.167 --> 00:07:48.300
I mean, I am talking about her
on national television.

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:50.200
-Yeah.
This is an outlier.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:53.367
-But it's not a big deal
until we go out to eat,

00:07:53.367 --> 00:07:56.267
and then I look like
the worst human on Earth,

00:07:56.267 --> 00:07:58.501
'cause my wife's like
[Imitates choking]

00:07:58.501 --> 00:08:00.501
And I'm like,
"This is good soup, huh?"

00:08:00.501 --> 00:08:02.200
[ Laughter ]

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:03.434
The waiter's like,
"Is she alright?"

00:08:03.434 --> 00:08:05.434
I'm like, "She's fine.
She had a brain tumor."

00:08:05.434 --> 00:08:07.234
You know?

00:08:07.234 --> 00:08:10.133
It probably looks like
I'm trying to kill her.

00:08:10.133 --> 00:08:14.167
You know, I'm like, "You got any
other stuff she can choke on?

00:08:14.167 --> 00:08:17.701
I just got her some of that
life insurance stuff."

00:08:17.701 --> 00:08:20.601
I'm a good husband.
-You are a good husband.

00:08:20.601 --> 00:08:22.601
I want to ask about this movie
real quick.

00:08:22.601 --> 00:08:23.834
Fantastic clip.

00:08:23.834 --> 00:08:25.801
Let me tell you,
sometimes the clips show up,

00:08:25.801 --> 00:08:28.200
and I don't feel like it tells
you enough about the movie.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:29.667
Because one
of the great details here

00:08:29.667 --> 00:08:31.534
is you are obviously
playing two roles in this film.

00:08:31.534 --> 00:08:33.234
-Yes.
-One is a sort of

00:08:33.234 --> 00:08:35.234
Bill Nye science guy.
-Yes.

00:08:35.234 --> 00:08:36.868
-You had to learn some science,
I guess --

00:08:36.868 --> 00:08:38.234
the text of science, at least.

00:08:38.234 --> 00:08:42.534
-And the other guy is an
astronaut, a former astronaut,

00:08:42.534 --> 00:08:46.534
and I know nothing about science
whatsoever.

00:08:46.534 --> 00:08:51.133
And so there was a challenge of,
like, not only do you have to --

00:08:51.133 --> 00:08:53.234
'Cause you have to learn lines

00:08:53.234 --> 00:08:57.334
and then you have to kind of,
you know, acquire an enthusiasm,

00:08:57.334 --> 00:09:00.767
and then I had to get to
this Bill Nye enthusiasm,

00:09:00.767 --> 00:09:04.033
and it was really hard
'cause I'm dumb.

00:09:04.033 --> 00:09:06.534
It's --
Science is just kind of --

00:09:06.534 --> 00:09:08.467
It's another language for me.

00:09:08.467 --> 00:09:11.834
So it was very difficult,
but I think I pulled it off.

00:09:11.834 --> 00:09:13.234
-I think you did a great job,
as well.

00:09:13.234 --> 00:09:15.334
And what a pleasure
to have you here, as always.

00:09:15.334 --> 00:09:18.534
Thank you so much.
You guys, that's Jim Gaffigan.

00:09:18.534 --> 00:09:20.667
"Linoleum" is
in select theaters now.

00:09:20.667 --> 00:09:22.767
For tour information,
go to jimgaffigan.com.

00:09:22.767 --> 00:09:24.300
Be right back
with more "Late Night."

